    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d202">

                <pb facs="0365" n="233" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv01/pg233"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 EW/7–845: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 202 <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d202-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgent—niact </hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-07-08T16:00:00+02:00">July 8, 1945—4
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>6877. Personal for the Secretary from Winant.</p>
                <p>The Dept’s 5473<anchor n="2" xml:id="d202-f2"/> and 5474<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d202-f3"/> (both July 5) were received here yesterday and
                    communicated to the FonOff. I had a brief talk with Mr. Eden over his private
                    wire. He volunteered the statement that he was very much pleased with the agenda
                    for the conference as forwarded. He is coming on to London for the first time in
                    some weeks Tuesday to take over his task as Acting Prime Minister because of Mr.
                    Churchill’s absence on vacation in France. I am <pb facs="0366" n="234"
                        xml:id="pg234"/> having dinner alone with him that night and after it will
                    forward you a more detailed account of the Brit reaction to the proposals we
                    have put forward.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d202-f4"/>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d202-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d202-f1-content">
                <p>Sent to Washington; relayed to Byrnes, then at sea, in telegram No. 8 of July, 9
                    (file No. 740.00119 (Potsdam)/8–645).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d202-f2" n="2" xml:id="d202-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <ref target="#d189">Document No. 189</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d202-f3" n="3" xml:id="d202-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed. This telegram instructed Winant to take the action reflected in <ref
                        target="#d231">document No. 231</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d202-f4" n="4" xml:id="d202-f4-content">
                <p>No report from Winant concerning such a dinner meeting has been found.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
